 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — the check exits non-zero when its own commands fail; large files only warn
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. A failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit: `git diff --cached` (carried
+  out of the `big=$(...)` assignment by `pipefail`) or `wc` on a staged file. Bash clears `-e` inside `$(...)` and
+  macOS `/bin/bash` 3.2 has no `inherit_errexit`, so the loop exits explicitly (`|| exit`) and behaves the same on 3.2
+  and newer bash. Large staged files stay a warning on stderr with exit 0. The size test is an `if`, so a file under
+  the limit is not a failure. `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` still skips staged-then-deleted files.
+- Forbidden: swallowing an internal error and still exiting 0 (`|| true`, `2>/dev/null` with a fallback, or dropping
+  `-e` or `pipefail`); making the large-file warning itself fatal; ending the loop body with a command that fails on
+  the normal path, such as `[ "$kb" -gt "$limit_kb" ] && echo`; a bare `shopt -s inherit_errexit`, which kills the
+  script on bash 3.2; removing the `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard.
+- Reason: on 2026-10-23 the maintainer overrode the 2026-09-11 fail-open decision (§check-fails-open: no `-e`, every
+  internal error ends in `exit 0`), so the check's own failures are no longer hidden behind exit 0. History: with
+  `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in the repository
+  for a day (2026-09-10). The accepted tradeoff: a broken check blocks commits again until it is fixed. The
+  `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard keeps deleted files from triggering that.
